138	
139	\Cref{tab:kstar_main} presents the central result. Across all three model families, $K^*=2$: the pairwise AUC between generation 1 and generation 2 ranges from 0.648 to 0.696 (above $\theta=0.60$), while AUC between generation 2 and generation 3 drops to 0.562--0.589 (below or near $\theta$). The JSD between consecutive generations decays by an order of magnitude from 0v1 (0.218--0.267) to 1v2 (0.012--0.016), and reaches a floor below 0.005 by generation 3.
140	
141	This consistency across architectures (GPT-NeoX, Transformer++, Qwen-style), training data (The Pile, Dolma, multilingual), and model sizes within the 1--2B range constitutes strong evidence that $K^*=2$ is a property of the scale regime rather than any specific model.
142	
143	\paragraph{Decoding Strategy Stability.} \Cref{tab:decoding} shows that $K^*=2$ is stable across four decoding strategies. Greedy decoding produces the largest initial JSD (0.302) because deterministic outputs diverge most from human text, yet $K^*$ remains 2. Only $T=1.2$ shows instability: one of three seeds yields $K^*=3$, as high-temperature sampling preserves more per-generation variance, delaying saturation. We conclude that $K^*=2$ is the default for standard decoding, with extreme temperature as the only identified modulating factor.
